To create a Flipper Zero app that allows you to store and boot a live ISO of Kali Linux from the Flipper as if it were a USB drive, you need to consider the following constraints and steps:

Key Considerations
	1.	Storage Limitation: Flipper Zero has limited internal storage (around 16MB) and cannot store a full Kali Linux ISO (~3GB+). You’d need an external storage solution (such as a microSD card or an OTG adapter for external USB storage).
	2.	USB Emulation: Flipper Zero can emulate various USB devices, but it does not function as a traditional USB mass storage device that allows booting.
	3.	Workarounds: Instead of directly booting Kali Linux from the Flipper, you might be able to:
	•	Use the Flipper to deliver a payload that enables network-based booting (PXE boot).
	•	Use the Flipper to act as a HID (keyboard/mouse) and execute scripts that download and write the ISO onto a USB drive dynamically.
	•	Store a minimal bootloader on the Flipper that then fetches the full ISO from another source.

Steps to Implement

1. Set Up the Flipper Zero Development Environment

You’ll need the ufbt framework to build and install Flipper apps.

git clone --recursive https://github.com/flipperdevices/flipperzero-firmware
cd flipperzero-firmware
./fbt

2. Explore USB Emulation Capabilities

Flipper Zero supports USB gadget mode via a custom firmware update. You might be able to:
	•	Configure it as a USB storage device.
	•	Emulate a USB flash drive with a bootloader.
	•	Act as a HID device to automate scripts on a target machine.

You can explore existing USB-related projects like:
	•	BadUSB scripts to execute automated actions.
	•	TinyUSB stack to modify and extend USB functionality.

3. Implement a Bootable USB Emulation

To create a bootable Flipper app:
	1.	Modify the USB descriptors to present the Flipper as a mass storage device.
	2.	Store a minimal bootloader or an iPXE image on the Flipper.
	3.	Redirect the boot process to fetch the Kali ISO from an external source.

Example USB mass storage descriptor:

const tusb_desc_device_t desc_device = {
    .bLength = sizeof(tusb_desc_device_t),
    .bDescriptorType = TUSB_DESC_DEVICE,
    .bcdUSB = 0x0200,
    .bDeviceClass = TUSB_CLASS_MASS_STORAGE,
    .bDeviceSubClass = MSC_SUBCLASS_SCSI,
    .bDeviceProtocol = MSC_PROTOCOL_BULK_ONLY,
    .bMaxPacketSize0 = CFG_TUD_ENDPOINT0_SIZE,
    .idVendor = 0xCAFE,
    .idProduct = 0x4001,
    .bcdDevice = 0x0100,
    .iManufacturer = 1,
    .iProduct = 2,
    .iSerialNumber = 3,
    .bNumConfigurations = 1
};

4. Load the Bootable ISO

Since Flipper has limited storage, you might:
	•	Use it to store a minimal bootloader like GRUB or iPXE.
	•	Use HID scripts to automate downloading and writing the ISO to a real USB drive.

Example HID script (BadUSB payload):

# Windows - Downloads Kali ISO and writes to USB
Start-Process "powershell" -ArgumentList "Invoke-WebRequest -Uri 'https://cdimage.kali.org/kali-latest.iso' -OutFile 'C:\kali.iso'"
Start-Process "diskpart" -ArgumentList "/s format_usb.txt"
Start-Process "powershell" -ArgumentList "dd if=C:\kali.iso of=\\.\PhysicalDrive1 bs=4M status=progress"

5. Flash and Test on Flipper

Once the firmware changes are implemented, compile and flash your modified firmware to the Flipper.

./fbt flash

Alternative Approach: PXE Boot

Instead of booting from a USB, the Flipper could:
	•	Emulate a USB NIC and trigger a PXE network boot.
	•	Deliver an attack payload that sets up PXE boot remotely.

Conclusion

While the Flipper Zero cannot directly act as a full USB boot device for a Kali ISO, it can be used in creative ways:
	•	Automate the ISO download and flashing to a USB drive.
	•	Emulate a USB network interface to trigger PXE boot.
	•	Act as a BadUSB device to execute scripts on the target system.

